<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Hungarian People's Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1949 CE – 1989 CE · capital Budapest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Communist Hungary, from Rákosi's Stalinism and the crushed revolution of 1956 to János Kádár's relatively relaxed 'goulash communism'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the communists eliminated their rivals in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-hungarian-republic">Second Hungarian Republic</a>, a Soviet-style constitution proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hungarian-democratic-republic">Hungarian People's Republic</a> on 20 August 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matyas-rakosi">Mátyás Rákosi</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-working-peoples-party">Hungarian Working People's Party</a>, ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinist</a> dictator, with show trials such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laszlo-rajk">László Rajk</a> and a feared secret police, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-protection-authority">ÁVH</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>'s death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-nagy">Imre Nagy</a> eased repression, and on 23 October 1956 a popular revolution brought him back to power and declared neutrality. Soviet forces crushed it in November, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janos-kadar">János Kádár</a> ruled for over thirty years. After harsh reprisals, Kádár allowed a mixed economy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-economic-mechanism">New Economic Mechanism</a> of 1968, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> the most liberal state in the Soviet bloc.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Economic problems and reformers in the party removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janos-kadar">Kádár</a> in 1988. In 1989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-round-table-talks">Round Table</a> talks agreed free elections, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-nagy">Imre Nagy</a> was solemnly reburied, and the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was opened. On 23 October 1989 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hungary">Republic of Hungary</a> was proclaimed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hungarian-peoples-republic.jpg" alt="Hungarian People's Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hungary_(1949-1956;_1-2_aspect_ratio).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1949 CE – 1989 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Budapest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mátyás Rákosi, Imre Nagy, János Kádár</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Protestant Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Communist Hungary</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1969&amp;focus=hungarian-peoples-republic" aria-label="Hungarian People's Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5125 512 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hungarian People's Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5445 768L5445 768M5445 768L5445 768"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5445 768h0M5445 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5445 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5445 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Budapest. <a href="/atlas?year=1969&amp;focus=hungarian-peoples-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
